{"author":{"id":"f943aa1c5ee078b881a8e97535a4e2ccb401d01ca00f4128126befa9cca58a32"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792326628,"nonce":"ISl/MHHomy7dJlnLLkyWLZ4cZ+s=","title":"Sync engine: send the labels git-bug changed, not the forge's whole set","message":"Left over from GB-6 (finding 7). The fork's engine (`bridge/core/syncpush.go`, pushChanges) sends labels with `SetLabels(git-bug's full set)` whenever git-bug's labels differ from what the forge last saw. A push without a pull first, which is git-board's **Send**, therefore removes labels added on the forge since the last Get or Sync.\n\ngit-board 27f3c20 only guards against it: Send is refused for a forge where the last Check found an issue changed on both sides. That guard has limits:\n- it knows only what a Check up to 15 minutes old saw;\n- a forge comment edit that comes without an event (GitLab, Gitea) slips through;\n- it refuses more than needed when an issue's only local change is new comments.\n\n**Fix in the fork:** send label changes against the base: labels added in git-bug since the base are added, labels removed are removed, and the forge's other labels are left alone. GitHub, GitLab and Gitea all have add/remove label calls; the synctest fake needs them too. Comment texts edited on both sides should become a conflict (as titles do), not an overwrite.\n\nThen git-board's Send guard can go (or stay as a warning), with a new fork tag and pin bump.","files":null}]}